        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What are the best gym accessories worth buying?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most home gyms: rubber flooring, an adjustable bench, a few resistance bands, a pull-up bar if you can mount one properly, collars for any barbell, and a jump rope if you have the space. Each lets you train more movements, train more safely, or protect the floor and equipment. Most other accessories are optional.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What should I buy first for a home gym?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Your main equipment first, then flooring, then a bench. Flooring protects the house from the start, and a bench roughly doubles what dumbbells or a barbell can do. Bands, a pull-up bar, collars and a rope follow. Buying a rack or storage before you own enough weights to need it is a common waste of space.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are horse stall mats good for a home gym?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, for most people. They are thick, dense recycled rubber at a low cost per square foot, and they handle dumbbells, benches and most barbell work. The downsides are weight, a strong smell when new and a textured surface. Air them out for several days before using them indoors, and consider tiles if you need to carry them upstairs.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a doorway pull-up bar safe?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be, if the door frame is solid, has moulding for the bar to bear against, and you stay within the maker's stated limit. Doorway bars can mark the frame and limit headroom, and weight limits vary widely between listings. A bar bolted into wall studs or ceiling joists, installed as the maker specifies, is stronger and more versatile.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need collars on a barbell at home?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Collars stop plates sliding off when the bar tilts, which can happen on any set, especially when pressing, racking or loading one side first. Use them every time, not just on heavy sets. Lever-style collars are quicker and hold more firmly than spring clips; check they fit your bar's sleeve diameter.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What home gym accessories are a waste of money?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sweat belts and sauna suits, electrical stimulation ab belts, thin foam puzzle mats used under heavy weights, wall timers for a single person, and gadgets that need a subscription for basic features are the categories we would skip. A storage rack bought before you own enough weights to fill it is another common one.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

